// SOLDER -- Stack Oriented Language for Deep Embedded Runtimes.
//
// A Forth in the broad sense: no syntax, postfix over a data stack, a dictionary of words that can be
// extended from the keyboard. What it adds to Forth is a *typed* cell -- integers of two widths, a
// double, a string, an array, an object, a colour, a moment, a coordinate -- and reference counting,
// so an array that goes out of use is freed at the point it stops being referred to rather than at a
// collection nobody scheduled.
//
// This package is the language itself and nothing else: it reads text and answers with text, and
// knows nothing about a board, a register or a terminal. What drives it is a separate program.
//
// This is the block a *consumer* writes to reach it:
//
//     dependencies {
//       solder { git = "github.com/sysl-lang/solder", version = "0.4.1" }
//     }
//
// The coordinate names the package, `solder`; the module a program imports is `sh.sysl.solder`.

package {
  name    = "solder"
  version = "0.4.1"

  // `from_utf8_unchecked` became an ordinary `sysl.text` function in 0.0.82, over a raw-tier
  // `str_cast`. It was a form in scope everywhere before that, so the four files here that reach it
  // gained an import and this floor is what says why an older compiler will not do.
  sysl    = "0.0.82"
}

// **`heap = true`, and it is a decision rather than an oversight.** The dictionary grows as words are
// defined, an array holds as many cells as it was asked to, and a string is as long as the person at
// the keyboard typed -- all three are sizes chosen at run time by somebody who is not the programmer.
// The board this is aimed at links a heap whether or not a program touches it, so the capability costs
// nothing that is not already spent.
//
// **What it buys that a fixed arena would not is the refcounting itself.** A cell holding an array is
// a counted reference, and the whole argument for this port is that the language does the retaining
// and releasing that `src/cell.c` does by hand in the C original.
requires {
  heap = true
}
